The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions on Haiti, in particular its resolutions 2476 (2019), 2653 (2022), 2692 (2023), 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023), 2743 (2024), 2751 (2024), and 2752 (2024),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and unity of Haiti,

Expressing grave concern over the continuing deterioration of the political, security, human rights, and humanitarian situation in Haiti, characterized by the expansion of armed gang violence, high rates of kidnappings, homicides, sexual and gender-based violence, recruitment and abuse of children, and attacks against critical national infrastructure and state institutions,

Stressing the severe regional implications of the ongoing crisis in Haiti, including mass displacement, humanitarian emergencies, and irregular migration flows that affect countries across the Americas and the Caribbean,

Emphasizing the vital importance of an inclusive, Haitian-led and Haitian-owned political process to restore democratic institutions, and urging all Haitian political and civil society actors, including the Transitional Presidential Council and the transitional Government, to urgently advance national dialogue, strengthen governance, restore the rule of law, and establish the necessary conditions and technical framework to hold free, fair, transparent, and credible legislative and presidential elections as soon as security conditions permit,

Welcoming the ongoing deployment and operations of the Multinational Security Support (MSS) mission authorized pursuant to resolution 2699 (2023) and renewed by resolution 2751 (2024), commending the contributing countries, and recognizing the critical role of international security assistance in supporting the Haitian National Police to restore public security and dismantle criminal gangs,

Expressing deep concern at the continued illicit trafficking of arms and ammunition into Haiti, which fuels armed gang violence and destabilization, and reiterating the obligation of all Member States to fully implement the targeted sanctions and the territorial arms embargo established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022) and renewed in resolution 2752 (2024),

Paying tribute to the dedication and efforts of the personnel of the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) under the leadership of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) until 15 July 2026, with its core mandate and reporting lines as set out in resolution 2476 (2019) and adjusted by resolutions 2692 (2023) and 2743 (2024);

2. Reaffirms the priority role of BINUH in facilitating an inclusive political dialogue, providing technical assistance to the transitional authorities and electoral bodies for the preparation of an inclusive, transparent, and credible electoral process, and assisting the Government of Haiti in strengthening the rule of law, judicial accountability, and the protection of human rights;

3. Calls upon all Haitian stakeholders to work collaboratively to advance the political transition, combat corruption, and ensure the functioning of state institutions, and urges the transitional authorities to make tangible progress toward convening national elections;

4. Encourages continued close coordination between BINUH, the MSS mission, the United Nations country team, regional partners, including the Caribbean Community (CARICOM) and the Organization of American States (OAS), and international donors, to maximize coherence in supporting the Haitian National Police and restoring stability;

5. Reiterates its call upon Member States to provide financial, logistical, and material support to the MSS mission and to the Haitian National Police, including through the United Nations Trust Fund for the MSS mission, to enable the mission to achieve its operational capacity;

6. Urges all Member States to take all appropriate and effective measures to stem the illicit trafficking and diversion of small arms, light weapons, and ammunition to Haiti, including by reinforcing border, maritime, and customs controls, and to cooperate fully with the Sanctions Committee and its Panel of Experts;

7. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Security Council every 90 days on the implementation of BINUH’s mandate, developments regarding the security and political situation, and the progress of the political transition towards elections;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions on Haiti, in particular its resolutions 2476 (2019), 2653 (2022), 2692 (2023), 2699 (2023), 2700 (2023), 2743 (2024), 2751 (2024), and 2752 (2024),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and unity of Haiti,

Expressing grave concern over the continuing deterioration of the political, security, human rights, and humanitarian situation in Haiti, characterized by the expansion of armed gang violence, high rates of kidnappings, homicides, sexual and gender-based violence, recruitment and abuse of children, acute food insecurity and malnutrition, and attacks against critical national infrastructure and state institutions,

Stressing the severe regional implications of the ongoing crisis in Haiti, including mass displacement, humanitarian emergencies, and irregular migration flows that affect countries across the Americas and the Caribbean,

Emphasizing the vital importance of an inclusive, Haitian-led and Haitian-owned political process to restore democratic institutions, strictly respecting the principles of national sovereignty, commending the ongoing good offices and facilitation efforts of the Caribbean Community (CARICOM), including its Eminent Persons Group, to support Haitian stakeholders in advancing political consensus, and urging all Haitian political and civil society actors, including the Transitional Presidential Council and the transitional Government, ensuring the full, equal, meaningful, and safe participation of women and the engagement of youth, to urgently advance national dialogue, strengthen governance, restore the rule of law, and establish the necessary conditions and technical framework to hold free, fair, transparent, and credible legislative and presidential elections as soon as security conditions permit,

Welcoming the ongoing deployment and operations of the Multinational Security Support (MSS) mission authorized pursuant to resolution 2699 (2023) and renewed by resolution 2751 (2024), commending the contributing countries, in particular the leadership of Kenya and the personnel contributed by CARICOM and African nations, and recognizing the critical role of international security assistance in supporting the Haitian National Police to restore public security and dismantle criminal gangs,

Expressing deep concern at the continued illicit trafficking of arms and ammunition into Haiti, which fuels armed gang violence and destabilization, calling upon Member States, particularly those in the region and countries of origin, to take effective measures to cut off illicit supply chains of arms and ammunition, and reiterating the obligation of all Member States to fully implement the targeted sanctions and the territorial arms embargo established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022) and renewed in resolution 2752 (2024),

Paying tribute to the dedication and efforts of the personnel of the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) under the leadership of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) until 15 July 2026, with its core mandate and reporting lines as set out in resolution 2476 (2019) and adjusted by resolutions 2692 (2023) and 2743 (2024);

2. Reaffirms the priority role of BINUH in facilitating an inclusive political dialogue, providing technical assistance to the transitional authorities and electoral bodies for the preparation of an inclusive, transparent, and credible electoral process, with the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and the engagement of youth, and assisting the Government of Haiti in strengthening the rule of law, judicial accountability, and the protection of human rights, with particular attention to addressing sexual and gender-based violence, grave violations against children, and combating impunity;

3. Calls upon all Haitian stakeholders to work collaboratively to advance the political transition, combat corruption, ensure the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and the engagement of youth, and ensure the functioning of state institutions, and urges the transitional authorities to make tangible progress toward convening national elections;

4. Encourages continued close coordination between BINUH, the MSS mission, the United Nations country team, regional partners, in particular the Caribbean Community (CARICOM) and its Eminent Persons Group, the Organization of American States (OAS), and the International Organization of La Francophonie (OIF), and international donors, to maximize coherence in supporting the Haitian National Police and restoring stability, while ensuring that security support integrates child protection and robust measures to prevent and address sexual and gender-based violence;

5. Reiterates its call upon Member States to provide predictable, sustainable, and flexible financial, logistical, and material support to the MSS mission and to the Haitian National Police, including through the United Nations Trust Fund for the MSS mission, to enable the mission to achieve its full operational capacity and ensure its long-term sustainability, notes with concern the funding shortfall affecting the MSS mission, and emphasizes the need for predictable and sustainable security support to Haiti;

6. Calls upon the international community, international financial institutions, and development partners to scale up humanitarian assistance, including by fully funding the United Nations Humanitarian Response Plan for Haiti, and to provide sustainable socioeconomic support to Haiti, addressing the root causes of poverty, acute food insecurity, and instability;

7. Urges all Member States, in particular countries of origin and transit, to take all appropriate and effective measures, in accordance with domestic law and international obligations, to stem the illicit trafficking and diversion of arms and related materiel of all types, including weapons and ammunition, to Haiti, including by reinforcing border, maritime, and customs controls in full accordance with international law, including international maritime law, and to cooperate fully with the Sanctions Committee and its Panel of Experts, and recalls the necessity of holding accountable and targeting through sanctions political and economic actors who support, finance, or arm criminal gangs;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Security Council every 90 days on the implementation of BINUH’s mandate, developments regarding the security and political situation, and the progress of the political transition towards elections;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

